e, model collapse) as the sameness mechanism — read each primary and say what it shows about whether the failure modes are fixed traits, training artefacts, or prompt-movable"}, {"key": "counter-cases", "prompt": "THE COUNTER-CASE that generated fiction can be good, and the publishing primaries: Jeanette Winterson's Guardian defence of the OpenAI metafiction story (March 2025); Rie Kudan's Akutagawa Prize novel (January 2024); Mark Lawrence's 2023 blind reader test; Vauhini Vara's 'Ghosts' (2021); Sudowrite's Muse claims as the vendor pole; Jane Friedman (August 2023); the Authors Guild 2023 survey; OneBookShelf's own policy text; Clarkesworld 'A Concerning Trend' (15 Feb 2023) and NPR's 2023 Clarke interview; Alex Hern's Guardian TechScape (May 2024) on the 'delve' dialect dispute — read the primaries, not the paraphrases"}, {"key": "critics-direct", "prompt": "CRITICS DIRECT, at the primary: Ted Chiang's New Yorker essay (31 Aug 2024) — the most-cited critic, so far seen only secondhand; Neil Clarke's own blog; Laura Preston's n+1 essay; Charlie Guo; Mohammad Siam; Ethan Mollick; Elizabeth Goodspeed; Nostalgebraist; Mark Williams / TNPS; Weixin Liang's self-edit study; plus Robin Sloan, John Scalzi, Chuck Wendig, Cory Doctorow, Tiffany Yates Martin, Victoria Strauss — what each says generated prose gets wrong in FICTION and what a human editor sees first"}], "baseIndex": 551, "existingHashes": ["b009e86b", "9a5050ec", "5884734b", "f1099bcb", "f1e55b50", "d1f4b30a", "b9776bfb", "2c1f3a2e", "d59f1a70", "47421233", "206a856c", "2a94c606", "80a4200a", "2debc70e", "d2e5931f", "15b5404c", "967be7e7", "54e1f52b", "34d2e55d", "e44838e9", "f44f1ab9", "b482ba60", "2bc300f4", "f9c6bd05", "f6c015ba", "2a762848", "872903cf", "7ac25e45", "e09535a9", "5431fff7", "d4a18f78", "99b8c426", "cec97c48", "83558261", "c068b50d", "19ca4b6", "3750c472", "565027c0", "88aafd65", "daf9ff16", "cc6b8184", "1013e320", "e0c1082f", "46c7047", "e1693eaa", "bd430864", "853f72df", "a768c768", "a8887